    {
      "who": "LEO",
      "text": "Mia, why do we get goosebumps?",
      "board": {
        "type": "title",
        "left": {
          "icon": "img:Human_physiology/Servier/skin",
          "label": "Goosebumps"
        },
        "right": {
          "icon": "❓",
          "label": "Why?"
        },
        "mid": "?"
      }
    },
    {
      "who": "MIA",
      "text": "Goosebumps are tiny bumps on your skin. Each little bump is around a hair.",
      "board": {
        "type": "big",
        "icon": "img:Human_physiology/Servier/skin",
        "text": "Tiny bumps on skin",
        "cue": "bumps",
        "sub": "One at each hair",
        "sub_cue": "hair"
      }
    },
    {
      "who": "LEO",
      "text": "Why are they called goosebumps?"
    },
    {
      "who": "MIA",
      "text": "Because they look like the bumpy skin of a goose, under all its feathers!",
      "punch": "goose",
      "board": {
        "type": "big",
        "icon": "🪶",
        "text": "Like goose skin!",
        "cue": "goose",
        "sub": "Under the feathers",
        "sub_cue": "feathers"
      }
    },
    {
      "who": "LEO",
      "text": "What makes them pop up?"
    },
    {
      "who": "MIA",
      "text": "Every hair has a tiny muscle at its root. When you feel cold, these muscles squeeze, and the hairs stand up.",
      "board": {
        "type": "journey",
        "title": "Goosebump time",
        "traveler": "🥶",
        "items": [
          {
            "icon": "🌬️",
            "label": "Feel cold",
            "cue": "cold"
          },
          {
            "icon": "img:Human_physiology/Servier/muscle-1",
            "label": "Muscles pull",
            "cue": "squeeze"
          },
          {
            "icon": "⬆️",
            "label": "Hair stands up",
            "cue": "stand"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "who": "LEO",
      "text": "How does that help?"
    },
    {
      "who": "MIA",
      "text": "For furry animals, it helps a lot! Fluffed-up fur traps warm air, like a puffy coat.",
      "board": {
        "type": "big",
        "icon": "🐱",
        "text": "Fluffy fur traps warmth",
        "cue": "fluff",
        "sub": "Like a puffy coat",
        "sub_cue": "puffy"
      }
    },
    {
      "who": "LEO",
      "text": "But I'm not very furry!",
      "punch": "furry"
    },
    {
      "who": "MIA",
      "text": "True! Our body hair is too thin to help. Scientists think goosebumps are left over from long ago, when our relatives were hairier.",
      "board": {
        "type": "big",
        "icon": "🕰️",
        "text": "Left over from long ago",
        "cue": "left",
        "sub": "Scientists think...",
        "sub_cue": "scientists"
      }
    },
    {
      "who": "LEO",
      "text": "Do I only get them when I'm cold?"
    },
    {
      "who": "MIA",
      "text": "Not just when you're cold! Feeling excited, surprised, or hearing music you love can do it too.",
      "board": {
        "type": "gallery",
        "title": "Goosebump times",
        "items": [
